  "summary_of_core_decisions": "The text employs a strict recursive dialectic where a thesis (Anselm's OA) is attacked by parodies (Gaunilo, Martin) exploiting content-neutrality, prompting a synthesis (Hartshorne's modal reformulation) that shifts the predicate from contingent to necessary existence. The reformulation is then tested against the same parodies, filtering them out via the modal axiom (S5/Becker's postulate) which excludes contingent objects. The argument is structurally sound in its recursive testing but relies heavily on the unproven acceptability of Becker's postulate and the symmetry-breaking between maximal good and maximal evil, leaving the final resolution truncated.",
